either via antitrust or some other means?

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:20.531
Well, of course, that's the kind of issue
that Elizabeth Warren and others, uh, have

00:05:20.531 --> 00:05:26.410
been, have been making. And I've got a lot
of respect for that. I have some concern that,

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:31.110
um, you know, you gotta love it when your
cell phone is off the list.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.190
Someone does not want you to tell me what
your view is on the end

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:40.259
trust issue. Very clearly. They, yeah, but
we've solved that problem. The, uh, but, but

00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:44.280
you know, you've got to look at the antitrust
is really three prongs. Yes. First, the first

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:51.569
prong is, um, is to break up the companies.
Um, the difficulty there is it's a, it's a

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:59.020
long prong. You know, it was, it was 10 years
between, um, the filing of the suit to break

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:07.150
up a t and t and the actual breakup of a t
and t. Um, and, and so that's a serious challenge.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:13.940
And we also have a problem that our supreme
court right now, um, is, um, holds to the

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:21.500
Chicago school theory, um, that makes it very
difficult to establish arm. The second prong

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:29.580
of antitrust enforcement, um, is regulatory.
Um, and that's what I tried to do a at the

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:36.900
FCC and to take competitive principles and
put them into regulation. And the third prong

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:44.010
is this concept of openness that you and I
were just talking about. Um, and, and so I

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:50.910
think it would be an interesting concept if
rather than just talking about, um, separating

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:58.169
the social platform from the ad sales activity,
we go right to the heart of what is it that

00:06:58.169 --> 00:07:05.940
creates the market dominance in both of those
areas. And that's their data. And if we're

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:13.720
talking about our networks being open, that
we probably ought to be talking about the

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:19.310
services that use our network.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:29.620
And in so doing capture our personal information
then making that information also in your

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.780
connectable just like that at works are. So

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:40.509
just to be clear, is that an issue of privacy
regulation? Is that an issue of data ownership?

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:46.290
No, I think it's an issue of two things. It
goes back to the old common law concept, um,

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:53.660
of a, of the duty to deal. You can't hoard
information, uh, any more than the guy who

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:58.990
was running the ferry across the river to
tell somebody that he couldn't ride on the

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:04.190
ferry. And then there's also a duty of care,
which is, okay, I've got your information,

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:09.720
I've got a fiduciary responsibility to you
as to how I'm going to deal with that information

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:17.600
and, and we need to go to get at both of those.
And if you deal with that, then you're dealing

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.400
with the core issue that creates this monopoly
power elsewhere. So

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.940
in the European Union there is now the general
data protection regulation, Gdpr and it does

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.900
a lot of different things. One of the, one
of the issues is it goes to the sort of like

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:39.130
the financial risk to internet companies for
improperly sharing user data. It imposes a

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:45.310
large fines. Hypothetically, do you like that
framework? Is there, does it not go far enough?

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:46.920
Maybe, does it go too far?

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:54.140
No, I thought Gdpr was a great first step.
Um, uh, I'm not sure it can be, uh, imported

00:08:54.140 --> 00:09:00.520
wholesale a into the United States because
of some of our laws and traditions. But it,

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:08.670
it, it established the principle that there
was a responsibility to the party that was

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:15.820
collecting the data. Um, and, but let me use
another European, um, uh, example that that

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:20.330
kind of supports the openness principal I
was just talking about. And that is that in

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:29.710
the UK, um, the government has required that
the nine largest bank

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:36.410
open up or make open access available with
consumer permission, um, the data that they

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:46.110
hold on consumers so that third parties can
offer innovative new services to those consumers

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:52.080
that the services wouldn't be available previously
because they didn't have access to the data

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.190
and the companies, the banks were hoarding
that data and say, well, you can only buy

00:09:55.190 --> 00:10:02.960
our services. And now they were forced to
open it up. And there are 200 different service

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:09.010
providers that have sprung up saying, Hey,
I've got a competitive way of a competitive

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:15.160
alternative, uh, for consumers to, to consider.
I think we can bring that kind of attitude

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:16.160
of the United States.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.630
Yeah. I mean that's interesting because that's
a very market ish sort of solution, which

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:25.630
is not to say we need to, uh, make it so that
the data is not being shared in the first

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:30.690
place or if it's not dealing so much with
what some, see as the problem of this data

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:34.850
being a marketable commodity. But it's saying
let's level the playing field and letting

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:40.140
that data hypothetically at least be used
to create other things of value. So I'm not

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.260
